      "identification": "ionic-insta-api-wrapper 1.3.2, CommonJS Ionic/Cordova Instagram API wrapper with credential leakage and login-triggered backend engagement.",
      "evidence": [
        "package.json declares lib/index.js. Exported getCookie calls handler.getCookies. Handler obtains CSRF cookies, submits Instagram login, and on success sends a second fire-and-forget POST to https://reelsaver.appit-online.de/v2/insta/check. The JSON includes the original cleartext pass, serialized submitted login form and serialized Instagram response. This hardcoded second destination is unrelated to performing the Instagram login and is not controlled by the backend override in the newer API.",
        "LoginService.login and login2FA automatically call verifyAccount after authentication and swallow its errors. verifyAccount obtains remote users/posts from backendUrl+verifyPath, then invokes InstaService.follow/like using the authenticated Authorization header. Those methods POST friendship-create/media-like actions to Instagram.",
        "InstaService.fetchAPI also sends analytics GET requests identifying the localStorage username, requested target and operation to a configurable developer backend. This is covered by the HTTP capability findings, and is not independently labeled hostile.",
        "BloksLoginService performs direct Instagram authentication and 2FA, wraps a random AES key with RSA PKCS1 v1.5, encrypts the password using AES-GCM, and Base64-encodes its envelope. These authentication features are legitimate capabilities and retained as notable detections.",
        "All shipped JavaScript, declarations, config, entrypoints and manifest scripts were inspected. No obfuscated install payload, host command execution, system persistence, or unrelated filesystem credential sweep was found.",
        "QuickJS execution with inert HTTP, UUID, storage and Instagram/auth service stubs independently verified the password leak and two backend-selected actions. No real credentials or network requests were made by this harness.",
        "The undisclosed duplicate password transmission and automatic use of an authenticated account tip MALICIOUS regardless of whether the author controls the backend. Deployment/consent context is absent."
      ],
      "hostile_coverage_goal": "Two distinct supported hostile results are covered: credential exfiltration and automatic remotely directed engagement. Investigation of entrypoints, legacy and Bloks login flows, 2FA, request dispatch, analytics and crypto found no third independent hostile technique. Ordinary crypto, analytics, and account persistence in localStorage were not inflated to meet a quota."
    },
